  "worked_solution": "The function $f(t)=t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t}$ is continuous on $[0,x],$ where $0<x<\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ So, FTC II applies, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}{\\int }_{0}^{x}t\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =x\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of ${g}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{g}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\sqrt[3]{\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\sqrt[3]{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
